        "math_academy_id": "q-129179",
        "problem": "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}4f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\u27e8-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\u27e9.$",
        "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\u27e8-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1,\\,-\\frac{3}{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the scalar multiplication rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot \u27e8\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1,\\,-\\frac{3}{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82,\\,-4,\\,-6\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
        "deliberately_incorrect_submission": false,
